In a discussion about grammatical correctness, James sought clarification on two phrases. For the first, I confirmed that "the share of population within a bin who experience damages" is correct, emphasizing that "experience" aligns with "who" referring to the people, not "share." I suggested a smoother variant: "the share of the population within a bin that experiences damages." Next, James inquired about "the low income quartile bear an economic burden," to which I advised using "bears" when treating the quartile as a single group. I also proposed a more natural phrasing: "Households in the lowest-income quartile bear an economic burden."
